      {
        "id": 227784,
        "name": "Braithwhite",
        "username": "Braithwhite",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/braithwhite/{size}/1854_2.png",
        "created_at": "2019-06-09T16:12:48.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>It&nbsp;kind of depends on what your personal GM style is.&nbsp; If&nbsp;your players want to bash their way through everything, you can roll with it and make things more and more actiony/over the top... or start hitting them with unexpected negative consequences later because they bashed their way through something that could have given them an advantage. I feel like either is appropriate, as long as you aren't vindictive about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\n<p>On the practical side, one thing that you can do is start introducing challenges mid-combat- physically putting a card with checkboxes on the table that tells them what to do gives them a pretty big hint that something bad will happen if they choose not to do it!&nbsp; (For example, a challenge to save civilians that they choose to ignore could have the people of the city seeing them as the villains!)</p>",
        "post_number": 3,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T06:02:03.383Z",
